  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1220 AM CDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\nThe hottest day of this forecast is expected to be Thursday, when\na mid/upper level ridge shifts east over Texas, and when a strong\n850mb thermal ridge builds east over our area. This looks to be \naided by compressional warming effects with an approaching cold \nfrontal boundary from the north. During the day Thursday, a \nsurface low is forecast to move from near Lubbock south-southeast \nto near the western border of Sterling County. A dryline extending\nsouth from this low should mix east into Sterling, Irion and \nCrockett Counties. A relatively humid airmass will be situated \nover most of our area ahead of the dryline. The hottest \ntemperatures (approaching or reaching 110 degrees) are expected \nbehind the dryline, while the highest heat indices (approaching or\nreaching 110) should be in the eastern third of our area. A Heat \nAdvisory continues in effect for our area through Thursday (until \n1 AM Friday). Please follow heat safety precautions if you plan to\nbe outside. \n\nThe cold front is forecast to move south across the Big Country \nThursday night, and possibly as far south as a San Angelo to \nBrownwood line. On Friday the front is expected to continue moving \nsouth to I-10 by early afternoon, and sag just south of our area by \nearly Friday evening. The area will have low to medium chances \n(mostly 25-55 percent) of showers and thunderstorms, but the details \non placement and timing are currently uncertain. Some of the hi-res \nmodels indicate thunderstorm development in the vicinity of \nnorthwest Texas or our northern counties Thursday evening or early \nThursday night, and then move a cluster or complex of storms \nsoutheast across our area through the overnight hours. If this \noccurs and the storms have an organized cold pool, given the strong \ninstability on point forecast soundings in the area ahead of the \nstorms, strong or locally damaging winds will be possible. \nPrecipitable water values are forecast to increase to between 1.5 \nand 2 inches Thursday night and remain through Friday. Expect \nconvection on Friday to be slower moving, and coverage could be \nincreased if there is a weak, embedded disturbance aloft over the \narea. With some similarities to last weekend, heavy rainfall in\nshort periods of time and localized flash flooding will be \npossible. Low (15-20 percent) rain chances linger into Saturday \nfor southern and southeastern portions of our area. \n\nWith increased cloud cover, temperatures will be a little cooler on \nFriday. Highs Saturday are expected to be in the lower to mid 90s \nwith partly cloudy skies. Hotter temperatures to return for Sunday \nand Monday, when an east-west ridge aloft is forecast to become \nelongated from the northern Gulf across Texas and northern Mexico.\nHighs are forecast to be 100-105 degrees in some of the Big\nCountry and Concho Valley, and mid to upper 90s elsewhere.  \n\nThe northern third of the area will have a low to medium (20-40 \npercent) chance of showers and storms Monday night and Tuesday. This \nto occur with a cold front that may move as far south as the Big \nCountry. An upper trough is progged to develop southeast into the \nMidwest, and a shortwave trough is progged to move southeast across \nthe southern Plains and Red River Valley.",
